body:
On June 12th the Board of County Commissioners voted 4 to 1 to approve the master plan for Commonweal Conservancy's Village at the Galisteo Basin Preserve project. (Commissioner Virginia Vigil voted against it after hearing County Hydrologist Karen Torres's concerns about water.) Eight Galisteans attended. All speakers approved of Commonweal's concept.
Richard Griscom again presented the Planning Committee's conditions in regard to impact on our water supply. Thanks to a timely motion by Mike Anaya, all five were approved.
1) that the County require that the hydrological assumptions used and the testing carried out to estimate water availability for the project be as conservative and thorough as possible;
2) that the hydrological impact of the project be evaluated, by examining the water and water rights requirements of all three phases of the project, before final approval;
3) that Commonweal be required to show beyond a reasonable doubt that its Galisteo Basin Preserve will not cause an impairment of Galisteo's wells;
4) that Commonweal post a Performance Bond to guarantee that Galisteo's water supply be restored to its condition prior to the development if the water supply is impaired due to this development, as established by a joint monitoring program;
and
5) that if the Galisteo Basin Preserve ties in with EAWSD (Eldorado Water Association) for its water supply, the Lamy wells will not be drawn upon for the project's water needs.
The next step for Commonweal is to gain approval for their Development Plan.

Eric Thomas, elected last year as President of the Galisteo Community Association, has stepped down, having realized that he did not have the time to give his full attention to pending issues, including the formation of a Community Organization (see below), as he is engaged in an extensive art project. Treasurer Wayne King is replacing him in the interim.

At the regular bi-monthly Board meeting on January 2011 the GCA approved becoming a Community Organization (CO) in accordance with the recently adopted Santa Fe County Sustainable Land Development Plan (SLDC) -- the culmination of five years of meetings, deliberations and planning between county residents (including Galisteo) and the staff of the Board of County Commissioners (BCC). Galisteo residents (including Anna and Freddie Cardenas, Leslie Dilworth, Frank Hirsch, Lucy Lippard, Matthew McQueen, Barbara and Philip Pfeiffer, Maria Ortiz y Pino, and Roger Taylor, among others) spent years with county staff, creating the Galisteo Community Plan.

A CO is a quasi-governmental body within a defined geographic area in Santa Fe County that has an elected board of directors representing that particular community. The Galisteo CO's functions will be:

1. Review any proposed development plans in the Galisteo plan area;
2. Augment or request changes in the SLDC so as to conform with the Galisteo Community Plan;
3. Coordinate the above activities with the SF County staff, as well as any other issues that affect the community;
4. Present Galisteo issues before the BCC and hear BCC issues that may affect Galisteo;
5. Help prepare the annual Capital Improvement Project Plan and Capital Outlay requests that go before the state legislature;
6. Help plan the central business core of Galisteo, working with county staff through the Frontier Communities Program.

Becoming a CO ensures that Galisteo has more control over and input into issues that affect it, such as proposed land development, business activities, and other matters that can be inconsistent with its approved Community Plan. It gives Galisteo a direct link to the BCC, allowing us to have more control over our future. The next step will be to join or to form a Registered Organization, which would give us notification beyond the boundaries of the village as defined by the Community Plan.
